            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 18-year-old male patient, admitted on 31/12/2011 with a one-day history of periumbilical pain migrating to the right iliac fossa, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed localised tenderness at McBurney's point with guarding. Laboratory investigations confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids, commenced on analgesics and prophylactic antibiotics, and underwent an uncomplicated laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 01/01/2012. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was provided, and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient was mobilised early and discharged home on 05/01/2012 in good condition. He is advised to follow up with you in two weeks. Please do not hesitate to contact me with any questions.</div>"
            },
